From 19704c374e05d19b679a7536b0a03e3e371f7eab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arealjim <6187715+arealjim@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:49:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/21] steam-frame client: in-order frame queue, held FOV for
 the hidden keepalive, arrival and pacing counters

The render loop took only the newest decoded frame. The sender runs at exactly 72.0 Hz and the headset loop at 72.1, so their phase drifts through a period every two minutes; while it sits near the pickup boundary, the Mac pacing jitter (sleep overshoot 3 ms mean, 6 ms max) lands two frames in one headset period and the first was dropped - 12 to 28 pct repaints in that phase, measured 2026-10-18 over a lossless USB link, at every bitrate from 25 to 80 Mbps. FRAME_CLIENT_QUEUE (default 2) keeps a short in-order ring instead: the loop takes the oldest, a backlog past the depth is cut to the newest. Off-head A/B over the 6 GHz link: 0.0-0.7 pct repaints in every run with the queue, 0-28 pct without. FRAME_CLIENT_WAIT_MS (default 0) is a bounded wait for a late frame, kept as a knob.

The hidden-session keepalive sent whatever xrLocateViews returned: SteamVR gives an unworn headset a still pose but a field of view that changes on 712 of 720 frames (angleLeft wobbling -1.037..-1.013 rad), and the server projects every packet FOV raw - a zoom shimmer with a still pose. The last FOV the wearer saw is now held for the keepalive (the first hidden one before any wearer). Only valid poses are sent (re-stamped last valid otherwise).

Hidden loop logs every 10 s what a wearer would get: loops, fresh frames, would-be repaints, pts skips, pose steps, raw and sent FOV changes; the arrival line gives inter-arrival mean/sd/max, pairs under 7 ms, gaps over 20 ms, pts spacing and the queue counters. The worn pipeline report carries the same arrival line.

